<h1>Orcraft Endpoints</h1>
<p>An endpoint is a machine that executes steps on the controller's behalf. Registering them is a setup
  task - see <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/">Adding endpoints</a> - and this page is
  about the other half: how a test says which endpoints it needs, what gets sent to them, and how a step
  ends up running there.</p>

<p>Endpoints are configuration, not code. A suite developed entirely against <code>localhost</code>
  becomes a distributed suite by changing values in the run panel, with no edit to the test body.</p>

<h2 id="declaring">Declaring endpoint fields</h2>
<p>Every test config already declares <code>test_endpoint</code>, defaulting to <code>localhost</code>. A
  test that needs more than one, or a different kind of selection, declares its own:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyTestConfig(OrcraftTestConfig):
	test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Test Endpoint")
	aux_endpoints = OrcraftConfigField.MultiSelectEndpoints(default=("localhost",), label="Aux Endpoints")') }}

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>Endpoint</code></td>
      <td><code>str</code></td>
      <td>One endpoint. Declare several fields when a test needs distinct roles - a producer and a consumer, a host and a device.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>MultiSelectEndpoints</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>A set of endpoints used together within one run, iterated by the test itself.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>EndpointFanout</code></td>
      <td><code>tuple[str, ...]</code></td>
      <td>A set of endpoints that each get their own run. Selecting three endpoints spawns three test runs.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The difference between the last two is what happens when several endpoints are selected.
  <code>MultiSelectEndpoints</code> gives one run with several endpoints available to it - the test loops
  over them and decides what each one does. <code>EndpointFanout</code> gives several independent runs of
  the same test, one per endpoint, executing in parallel. Use fanout to run the same verification
  everywhere; use multi-select when the endpoints play different parts in one scenario.</p>

<p>Redeclaring <code>test_endpoint</code> is how a test changes its own dispatch model, because the base
  config already declares it as a plain <code>Endpoint</code>:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.EndpointFanout(
	default=("localhost",),
	label="Test Endpoint",
	shared=True,
)') }}

<h3 id="endpoint-options">Endpoint field options</h3>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Option</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>port</code></td>
      <td><code>50051</code></td>
      <td>Port used when the configured value carries no port of its own.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>secure_enabled</code></td>
      <td><code>None</code></td>
      <td>Forces mutual TLS on or off for this field. <code>None</code> follows the endpoint's own registration.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>shared</code></td>
      <td><code>False</code></td>
      <td>Lets concurrent runs target the same endpoint at once instead of queueing for it.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>By default an endpoint is held for the duration of a run, and another run wanting the same endpoint
  waits its turn - which is what you want when a test owns a device. <code>shared=True</code> lifts that,
  treating each run as an independent client, which is what you want when several fanout-expanded runs
  legitimately share one host.</p>

<h2 id="dispatch">Dispatching steps</h2>
<p>Steps are the unit of remote execution. A <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/">flow</a>
  chooses where each one runs:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('produce_result = self.run_step(
	MyProduceStep,
	endpoint=self.config.endpoint_1,
	payload_size=self.config.payload_size,
)

consume_result = self.run_step(
	MyConsumeStep,
	endpoint=self.config.endpoint_2,
	input_file=transferred_path,
)') }}

<p>With no <code>endpoint</code> argument, a step runs on the endpoint the test is configured for. The
  framework establishes the connection, sends the step and its config, executes it there, and returns the
  result - so from the flow's point of view a remote step and a local one are the same call.</p>

<p>Because the step may run on another machine, everything it needs must arrive through its config, and
  everything it produces must leave through its result. A step cannot reach back into the test.</p>

<h2 id="packages">Uploading the test package</h2>
<p>Before a step runs remotely, the endpoint needs the code. The controller packages the suite and
  uploads it, governed by <code>upload_test_package</code>:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Upload on every run. The safe choice while the suite is changing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>if_missing</code></td>
      <td>Upload only when the endpoint does not already have the package. The default.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Never upload - the endpoint is expected to have the package already.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Data files are a separate matter from code. A file a test needs on an endpoint is declared as a
  package field, and the <code>endpoints</code> option binds it to the endpoint fields that actually need
  it:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('input_file = OrcraftConfigField.PackageFile(
	default="",
	label="Input File",
	required=True,
	endpoints=[endpoint_1],
)

input_folders = OrcraftConfigField.PackageFolderList(
	default=(),
	label="Input Folders",
	required=True,
	endpoints=[test_endpoint, aux_endpoints],
)') }}

<p>Inside the step, the value resolves to the path on the machine the code is executing on. Without
  <code>endpoints</code>, a package field is uploaded to every endpoint the test uses - fine for a small
  binary, wasteful for a large data set that only one endpoint reads. The field types are listed in
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#package-types">Configuration fields</a>.</p>

<h3 id="endpoint-app">Updating the endpoint application</h3>
<p><code>update_endpoint_app</code> decides whether the endpoint application itself is brought up to date
  before the run:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Value</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>always</code></td>
      <td>Update the endpoint application before every run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>on_mismatch</code></td>
      <td>Update only when the endpoint application does not match the controller. The default for a test.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>never</code></td>
      <td>Leave the endpoint application as it is.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>defer</code></td>
      <td>Express no opinion and leave the decision to the level below. The default for a suite or group.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="transfer">Transferring files between endpoints</h2>
<p>Package upload sends files from the controller. Moving files <em>between</em> endpoints during a run -
  produce on one machine, verify on another - is what a flow's <code>transfer</code> is for:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('transfer_result = self.transfer(
	produce_result.output_file,
	src_endpoint=self.config.endpoint_1,
	dst_endpoint=self.config.endpoint_2,
)

if transfer_result:
	return

consume_result = self.run_step(
	MyConsumeStep,
	endpoint=self.config.endpoint_2,
	input_file=transfer_result.dst_paths[0],
)') }}

<p><code>paths</code> takes a single file or folder path, or a list of file paths, and they travel as one
  zip archive rather than as a call per file. The returned object is truthy when the transfer failed,
  matching the <code>handle_errors</code> convention, and <code>dst_paths</code> holds one absolute path
  per element of <code>paths</code>, pointing at where each landed on the destination.</p>

<h2 id="state">Endpoint state</h2>
<p>Tests that deliberately disrupt an endpoint - a reboot, a service restart, a network drop - need to
  wait for it to come back. <code>OrcraftEndpointState</code> names the three states:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>State</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>OFFLINE</code></td>
      <td>The endpoint is not reachable.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>ONLINE</code></td>
      <td>The endpoint answers, but is not yet able to accept work.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>READY</code></td>
      <td>The endpoint is reachable and ready to execute steps.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import OrcraftEndpointState

...

self.run_step(
	MyRebootStep,
	endpoint=endpoint,
	expect_endpoint_disconnect=True,
)

if self.wait_for_endpoint_state(
	OrcraftEndpointState.OFFLINE,
	endpoint=endpoint,
	timeout_sec=self.config.disconnect_timeout_sec,
):
	return

if self.wait_for_endpoint_state(
	OrcraftEndpointState.READY,
	endpoint=endpoint,
	timeout_sec=self.config.connect_timeout_sec,
	poll_interval_sec=5,
):
	return') }}

<p><code>wait_for_endpoint_state</code> returns truthy on failure, like the other flow helpers, so
  <code>if ...: return</code> reads as the error path throughout. <code>restart_endpoint</code> wraps the
  whole sequence - restart, wait for the disconnect, wait for readiness - for the common case.</p>

<p>The step that causes the disconnection must be marked <code>expect_endpoint_disconnect=True</code>.
  Without it, the connection dropping mid-step is exactly what an infrastructure failure looks like, and
  the framework reports it as an error.</p>

<h2 id="security">Security</h2>
<p>Communication with an endpoint is secured with mutual TLS and token-based authentication, negotiated
  from how the endpoint was registered with the controller. Tests do not handle credentials: the security
  context follows the endpoint field, and flows and steps - including those called from
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/lifecycle-hooks/">lifecycle hooks</a> - inherit it
  automatically.</p>

<p><code>secure_enabled</code> on an endpoint field overrides the negotiated setting for that field, which
  is mainly useful for tests that exist to verify the secured and unsecured paths themselves.</p>
